  {
    "date": "2022-03-05",
    "term": "瓦",
    "kana": "かわら",
    "meaning": "roof tile",
    "level": "N2",
    "pos": ["noun (common) (futsuumeishi)"],
    "priority": ["ichi1", "news1", "nf09"],
    "pitch": [0],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["sound-change", "unclear"],
    "headline": "The source says 瓦, “roof tile”, is probably from Sanskrit, possibly via Middle Chinese.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "/kapara/ → /kaɸara/ → /kawara/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "Probably from Sanskrit कपाल (kapāla, “cup, bowl, skull”), possibly via Middle Chinese 迦波羅 (MC kae pa la). Along with Buddhism, roof tiles came to Japan in the Asuka period."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Cognate with 骨, 䯊 (kawara, “a bone, particularly a covering bone such as a skull or kneecap”)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Folk etymologies include:"
      },
      {
        "quote": "* From 変わら (kawara), the 未然形 (mizenkei, “incomplete form”) of verb 変わる (kawaru, “to change”), from the idea that tiles were produced by changing the clay into pottery through firing"
      }
    ],
    "wiktionaryDump": "2026-09-02"
  },
